est no-huddle rate and the 3rd highest RPO rate in the league. Cam Ward has run the passing game and leaned heavily on Carnell Tate, who has drawn 26% of the targets.","defense":"The Titans defense has been well below average by EPA allowed per play so far. Its strongest area has been sack rate, where it ranks 11th, and its weakest has been EPA allowed per rush, where it ranks 25th. Jeffery Simmons has been as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise is Jacob Martin, who has 3.5 sacks while playing only 58% of the snaps, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Their scheme has been fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature is a number of defenders in the box that ranks 5th lowest in the league.","special":"The Titans have been one of the most conservative teams on fourth down, going for it on only 0% of their short-yardage chances. Their kickers have made 91% of field goals, which is well above average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the defensive line, about 25% of the money on the books. Their linebackers were among the youngest in the league.","players":"Cam Ward has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 1,179 yards. Carnell Tate has been the clear top target with 26% of the looks and 413 receiving yards. Tony Pollard has handled most of the work in the backfield with 79% of the carries. On defense, Jeffery Simmons leads the team with 4 sacks and Amani Hooker has picked off 2 passes.","coaching":"Brian Daboll is calling the offense and Robert Saleh is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2025 Giants under Mike Kafka and the 2023 Cardinals under Drew Petzing. Their defense looks most like the 2023 Bears and the 2025 Eagles under Vic Fangio.","trends":"The offense went from 30th in EPA per play in 2025 to 31st so far this season, about where it was. The defense moved from 28th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 24th so far this season, a modest improvement. The biggest change in approach was fourth down go rate, which went from 50% to 0%.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}